Principles concerning the management of electricity markets

Member States, regulatory authorities, transmission system operators, distribution system operators, market operators and delegated operators shall ensure that electricity markets are managed in accordance with the following principles:

(a) price formation shall take place on the basis of supply and demand;

(b) market rules shall encourage free price formation and avoid actions that prevent price formation on the basis of supply and demand;

(c) market rules shall facilitate the development of more flexible generation, sustainable low-carbon generation and more flexible demand;

(d) final customers shall be enabled to benefit from the opportunities offered by the market and from increased competition in retail markets, and shall be given the possibility of acting as market participants in the energy market and in the energy transition;

(e) final customers and small enterprises shall be enabled to participate in the market by means of the aggregation of generation from multiple electricity generation installations or load from multiple demand response installations, thereby allowing for joint offers on the electricity market and joint management in the electricity system, in accordance with Union competition law;

(f) market rules shall enable the decarbonisation of the electricity system and thereby of the economy, including by making possible the integration of electricity from renewable energy sources and by providing incentives for energy efficiency;

(g) market rules shall provide appropriate investment incentives for generation, in particular long-term investments for a carbon-free and sustainable electricity system, energy storage, energy efficiency, and demand response, thereby meeting the needs of the market,

